jactionlint(1) -- static checker for GitHub Actions workflow files
=================================================================

## SYNOPSIS

`jactionlint` [<options>] <br>
`jactionlint` [<options>] <file>... <br>
`jactionlint` [<options>] `-`

## DESCRIPTION

`jactionlint` checks GitHub Actions workflow files for mistakes and insecure constructs. It validates the workflow syntax, types the `${{ }}` expressions, compares the `with:` inputs and the `steps.<id>.outputs` of actions with their metadata, runs `shellcheck` and `pyflakes` over the `run:` scripts, validates glob patterns, `needs:` dependencies, runner labels and cron schedules, and applies security and policy rules such as script injection, unpinned actions, excessive permissions and missing timeouts. It also checks the composite actions and the Dependabot configuration of the repository, and the Actions lock file against the workflows. Every finding carries the stable ID of the rule that reports it, such as `unpinned-uses`.

The rules form three profiles, and each profile includes the one before it. `correctness` has what actionlint checks and the bug detectors of jactionlint. `default` adds the security posture and policy rules, and applies when nothing is configured. `pedantic` adds the noisy and opinionated rules. Rules that no profile enables run only when the configuration turns them on, and the rules of the online checks run only when you turn those checks on with `--online`, `online: true` in the config file or `JACTIONLINT_ONLINE` (see the GitHub API section). Choose a profile with `--profile` or with `profile:` in the config file.

Without file arguments `jactionlint` checks the repository that holds the current directory. That is the nearest directory upwards that has a `.git` (a directory, or a file in a worktree or a submodule) or a `.jj` entry and that has a `.github/workflows` directory, a `.github/actions` directory or an `action.yml` or `action.yaml` in its root. When there is none, it exits with status 3. It then checks these files:

  * The workflow files: the `.yml` and `.yaml` files directly in `.github/workflows`. GitHub does not load files in subdirectories of it, so `jactionlint` checks them only when you name them as arguments.

  * The composite actions: every file named `action.yml` or `action.yaml` except one directly in `.github/workflows`, wherever it is in the repository. The directories `.git`, `node_modules`, `vendor`, `testdata`, `fixtures`, `.venv`, `venv`, `target` and `dist` are not searched, but an action in one of them is checked when a local `uses: ./path` refers to it.

  * The Dependabot configuration: `.github/dependabot.yml` or `.github/dependabot.yaml`.

  * The Actions lock file `.github/workflows/actions.lock`, when it exists.

Nothing uses the network unless you give `--online`, set `online: true` in the config file or set `JACTIONLINT_ONLINE` in the environment.

## OPTIONS

### Option syntax

  * A long option is `--name`. It takes its value as `--name=VALUE` or `--name VALUE`.

  * A short option is one letter. It can be bundled with others and takes its value attached or separate: `-vfjson`, `-cfile`, `-c file`. Only the most used options have one.

  * `--` ends the options. A lone `-` is not an option: it means stdin.

  * An option whose value is optional (`--color`, `--fix`, `--online`, `--baseline`, `--baseline-write`) takes it only as `--name=VALUE`. `--fix unsafe` is `--fix` plus a file named `unsafe`.

  * Abbreviations of long options are not accepted. An unknown option or an invalid value exits with status 2. The old single-dash spellings of long options, as actionlint writes them, are rejected, and the error names the replacement.

### General

  * `-h`, `--help`:
    Print the help and exit with status 0.

  * `-V`, `--version`:
    Print the version and how this binary was installed, then exit.

  * `-v`, `--verbose`:
    Print verbose logs to stderr, such as the repository that was found, every online lookup that was skipped and which source supplied the token.

  * `--debug`:
    Print debug logs to stderr. They are meant for the development of `jactionlint`.

  * `--completion`=<SHELL>:
    Print the completion script of a shell (`bash`, `zsh`, `fish` or `powershell`; `pwsh` is accepted as `powershell`) and exit. The script completes the options, their values and the rule IDs of this version.

  * `--stdin-filename`=<NAME>:
    File name used in the output when reading stdin (default `<stdin>`). The name also decides what stdin is: a `dependabot.yml` or `dependabot.yaml` without a directory or in a `.github` directory is Dependabot configuration, an `action.yml` or `action.yaml` is the metadata of an action, `.github/workflows/actions.lock` is the Actions lock file, and anything else is a workflow. When the name is the path of an existing file in a repository, the config file of that repository applies to the input; otherwise the user-global config file or the defaults apply.

### Output

  * `-f`, `--format`=<FORMAT>:
    Output format: `text` (default), `oneline`, `json`, `jsonl`, `sarif`, `gcc`, `github`, `summary` (counts per rule and per file) or a Go template that contains `{{ }}`, such as `'{{json .}}'`. The page https://jactionlint.jdx.dev/output#format-error-messages describes the fields of each format.

  * `--oneline`:
    One line per finding. The same as `--format oneline`.

  * `--rule-ids`:
    Accepted for compatibility. The text format always shows the rule ID at the end of each finding, because the configuration, `--ignore` and the ignore comments take it.

  * `--color`[=<WHEN>]:
    Colorize the output: `always`, `never` or `auto` (default), which colors the output when it goes to a terminal. A bare `--color` means `always`. `NO_COLOR` turns the colors off in every mode (see ENVIRONMENT).

  * `--no-color`:
    The same as `--color=never`.

  * `--show-doc-urls`:
    Print the URL of the documentation of the rule of each finding: under the finding in the text format (not with `oneline`) and in the message of the annotation in the `github` format.

  * `--finding-context`:
    Add `events` (the events of `on:` of the workflow) and `permissions` (the permissions of the token of the job the finding is in, as a map of each scope to `read`, `write`, `none` or `unknown`, where `*` stands for all scopes) to each finding of the `json`, `jsonl` and `sarif` formats. `permissions` is left out when the file sets none. Without this option neither is in the output.

  * `--no-hints`:
    Do not print the hint line after a run with the `text` or `oneline` format that reports 20 or more findings with a profile other than `correctness`. The line goes to stderr, only when stderr is a terminal or `CI` or `GITHUB_ACTIONS` is set (see ENVIRONMENT), and never with another format. It says how to count the findings (`--format summary`), how to adopt them gradually (`--baseline-write`) and how to get the checks of actionlint only (`--profile correctness`). The option also silences the notes that `shellcheck`, `pyflakes` or `node` was not found (see the External tools section). `JACTIONLINT_NO_HINTS=1` does the same.

  * `--min-severity`=<LEVEL>:
    Hide the findings less severe than LEVEL: `info` (default), `warn` or `error`. Hidden findings do not count for the exit status.

  * `--strict-exit`:
    Exit with status 1 for a finding of any level. By default only a finding of level `error` does.

### Rules and configuration

  * `-p`, `--profile`=<NAME>:
    Rule profile: `correctness`, `default` or `pedantic` (see DESCRIPTION). It overrides `profile` of the config file. The former names `strict` and `all` are accepted in the config file only and mean `pedantic`.

  * `-c`, `--config-file`=<FILE>:
    Read this file instead of the config file of the repository and the user-global one (see FILES). A file that it names under `extends` is still read.

  * `-i`, `--ignore`=<PATTERN>:
    Ignore the findings whose rule ID or message matches PATTERN, which is a rule ID such as `unpinned-uses` or a regular expression. The option is repeatable: `--ignore A --ignore B` ignores the findings that match A or B. It takes one pattern, so a comma does not separate two. To accept a finding in one place, use an ignore comment (`# jactionlint ignore=<PATTERN>` above the line or at its end) or an entry of `ignores:` in the config file. The entry matches by rule, file, job, step and `uses:`, survives a tool such as Renovate rewriting the line, and can expire (`expires: YYYY-MM-DD`). See https://jactionlint.jdx.dev/ignore.

  * `--init-config`:
    Write a default config file to `.github/jactionlint.yaml` of the repository and exit. It exits with status 3 and writes nothing when the file exists.

  * `--migrate-config`:
    Rewrite the deprecated keys of the config file (`.github/jactionlint.yaml` or the file of `--config-file`) into the `rules` mapping, and exit.

  * `--migrate-ignores`:
    Rewrite the trailing `# zizmor: ignore[...]` comments of the files into `# jactionlint ignore=...` comments, and exit. Without file arguments it takes the workflow files and the root `action.yml` or `action.yaml` of the repository. `jactionlint` also honors the zizmor comments as they are.

  * `--select`=<RULES>:
    Report only the findings of these rules, given as comma separated rule IDs, also of the rules that the profile does not enable. The option is repeatable. A level that the configuration sets for a selected rule stays, and a selected rule that the configuration turns off runs at its default level. `shellcheck` and `pyflakes` run only when they are selected.

  * `--no-ignores`:
    Apply no ignore comment, no `ignores` and `paths` ignore of the config file and no baseline, so that the run shows what they hide. The `--ignore` options stay. The option cannot be combined with `--baseline`, `--baseline-check` and `--baseline-write`.

  * `--list-rules`:
    List every rule ID with its group, the profile that enables it, its default level and a summary, and exit. The profile is `correctness`, `default` or `pedantic`, `online` for a rule of the online checks, or `opt-in` for a rule that runs only when configured. With `--format json` or `jsonl` the list is JSON.

  * `--print-config-schema`:
    Print the JSON Schema of the config file, with the rule IDs and the options of each rule, and exit. `--init-config` writes a comment that points editors to it. The option cannot be combined with `--print-output-schema`.

  * `--print-output-schema`:
    Print the JSON Schema of the output of `--format json` and `jsonl`, and exit.

  * `--explain`=<RULE>:
    Print the documentation of a rule ID and exit: summary, group, profile, default level, options, fixes and its section of the checks page. The text is part of the binary, so the option needs no network.

### Fixing

The fixes are safe or unsafe. A safe fix keeps the behavior of the workflow. The page https://jactionlint.jdx.dev/fix lists the rules that have one.

  * `--fix`[=`unsafe`]:
    Apply the safe automatic fixes to the files and report what remains. `--fix=unsafe` also applies the fixes that may change the behavior of the workflow. `jactionlint` checks every pass: the result must be valid YAML that differs from the original only where the edits are, or it refuses the fix and exits with status 3. It exits with status 1 when a finding of level `error` remains. `--fix` cannot read stdin.

  * `--diff`:
    Print the changes `--fix` would make as a unified diff on stdout and write no file. It implies `--fix`. The findings that remain go to stderr. It exits with status 1 when there is a diff or a finding of level `error` remains.

  * `--fix-rules`=<RULES>:
    With `--fix` or `--diff`, apply only the fixes of these comma separated rule IDs. The `fix.rules` key of the config file does the same. Without `--fix` or `--diff` the option exits with status 2.

### Baseline

A baseline records the findings that exist today, so that later runs fail only on new ones. The page https://jactionlint.jdx.dev/baseline describes the procedure.

  * `--baseline`[=<FILE>]:
    Hide the findings recorded in the baseline file, which is `.github/jactionlint-baseline.json` in the repository unless FILE names another. The baseline is used only when this option is given or the config file has `baseline: auto` (`true` and a file path work too, `false` turns it off). The exit status counts only the findings that are not in the baseline.

  * `--no-baseline`:
    Ignore the baseline, also when the config file enables it. The option cannot be combined with `--baseline`.

  * `--baseline-write`[=<FILE>]:
    Record the current findings as the baseline and exit with status 0. The command prints the line to put in the config file so that every run uses the baseline. With file arguments it refreshes only the entries of those files. The file has no line numbers, so writing it again without a change in the findings gives the same bytes. Write it in the environment that CI runs in: with the same configuration, with `--online` if CI uses it, and with the same `shellcheck` and `pyflakes`. A finding that your machine cannot produce is not recorded, and CI would report it as new. The option cannot be combined with `--baseline`, `--no-baseline`, `--baseline-check`, `--fix` or stdin.

  * `--baseline-check`:
    Report the baseline entries that match no finding any more as `unused-baseline-entry` (level `info`). It implies `--baseline`.

  * `--sarif-hide-baselined`:
    Leave the findings that the baseline accepts out of `--format sarif`. By default they are in the log as results with a suppression of the kind `external`.

### GitHub API (online checks)

The online checks ask the GitHub API about the actions that a workflow uses. The rules that need them show `online` as their profile in `--list-rules` and `only with --online` on https://jactionlint.jdx.dev/rules. Without `--online` nothing uses the network.

The token is the first of these: the variable named by `--online-token-env`, the file named by `--online-token-file`, `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `GH_TOKEN` (on a GitHub Enterprise Server `GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN` and `GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN` come before these two) and the output of `gh auth token` when the `gh` command is installed. `jactionlint` sends it only to the API host. The page https://jactionlint.jdx.dev/online describes the checks, the cache and the rate limits.

  * `--online`[=<MODE>]:
    Run the online checks. They run the rules that ask GitHub about an action (for example `archived-uses`, `impostor-commit` and `known-vulnerable-actions`), check the inputs, the secrets and the outputs of remote actions and reusable workflows against their metadata, and let `--fix` replace a tag by the commit it points to (`--fix=unsafe` also pins container images to digests). MODE is `cache` (answer from the disk cache only and never use the network), `strict` (exit with status 3 when a lookup had to be skipped) or `cache,strict`. A lookup that fails (404, 403, server error, timeout, rate limit) is skipped with one warning per kind of failure and does not change the exit status, unless `strict` is given. The environment variable `JACTIONLINT_ONLINE` sets the default.

  * `--no-online`:
    Never use the network, even when the config file has `online: true` or `JACTIONLINT_ONLINE` asks for the online checks. The option cannot be combined with `--online`.

  * `--online-api-url`=<URL>:
    The REST API of a GitHub Enterprise Server, such as `https://ghe.example.com/api/v3`. The option wins over `online-options.api-url` of the config file. Without both, the URL is `$GITHUB_API_URL`, else derived from `$GITHUB_SERVER_URL` or `$GH_HOST`, else `https://api.github.com`. A host that only the config file of the repository names gets no token.

  * `--online-token-env`=<NAME>:
    The name of the environment variable that holds the GitHub token. It is read before `GITHUB_TOKEN` and `GH_TOKEN`.

  * `--online-token-file`=<FILE>:
    The file that holds the GitHub token. It is read before `GITHUB_TOKEN` and `GH_TOKEN`.

  * `--online-github-com-fallback`:
    On a GitHub Enterprise Server, look up on `api.github.com` the repositories that the Server answers 404 for (actions that GitHub Connect syncs from github.com). The token for github.com is `GITHUB_COM_TOKEN` or the one named by the next two options, and the token of the Server is never sent there. The names of those repositories go to github.com, so keep internal ones out with `--online-deny`.

  * `--online-github-com-token-env`=<NAME>:
    With the fallback: the name of the environment variable that holds the token for `api.github.com` (default `GITHUB_COM_TOKEN`).

  * `--online-github-com-token-file`=<FILE>:
    With the fallback: the file that holds the token for `api.github.com`.

  * `--online-allow`=<PATTERN>:
    Look up only the repositories that match the `owner/repo` PATTERN, where `*` is a wildcard. The option is repeatable, and a value may hold several comma separated patterns.

  * `--online-deny`=<PATTERN>:
    Never look up the repositories that match the `owner/repo` PATTERN, for example `--online-deny 'mycorp/*'` for private actions. The option is repeatable, and a value may hold several comma separated patterns. Deny wins over allow.

  * `--online-cache-ttl`=<DURATION>:
    How long an answer of the GitHub API is used from the disk cache without asking GitHub whether it changed (default `1h`). `0` checks every answer.

  * `--online-report`=<FILE>:
    Write how complete the online checks were to FILE as JSON, whatever the `--format`. It has the `status` (`complete`, `incomplete` or `disabled`), the skipped lookups by kind, the excluded repositories and where the answers came from (network or cache, with the age of the oldest cached one).

  * `--online-max-wait`=<DURATION>:
    The longest to wait for a GitHub rate limit to reset (default `30s`). A limit that resets later skips the lookups. `0` never waits.

### External tools

`jactionlint` runs `shellcheck` over the shell scripts and `pyflakes` (or `ruff`) over the Python scripts of `run:`. It runs `node` (version 20 or later) for the rule `github-script-syntax`, which has no option, and `git` to list the files of the repository for the rule `unmatched-paths-filter`. When the default `shellcheck` or `pyflakes`, or `node`, is not found, `jactionlint` skips the scripts and prints one note on stderr for each missing tool that says how many scripts it did not check. `--no-hints` silences the notes.

  * `--shellcheck`=<CMD>:
    The command or file path of `shellcheck` (default `shellcheck`). An empty value disables the integration. A command given here must exist: the exit status is 3 when it is not found or not executable.

  * `--pyflakes`=<CMD>:
    The command or file path of `pyflakes` (default `pyflakes`). An empty value disables the integration. A command given here must exist: the exit status is 3 when it is not found or not executable.

  * `--ruff`=<CMD>:
    Check the Python scripts of `shell: python` with this `ruff` instead of `pyflakes`. It runs only its pyflakes rules (`F`) and ignores the configuration of the working directory. The command must exist: the exit status is 3 when it is not found or not executable.

  * `--shellcheck-rcfile`=<FILE>:
    Let `shellcheck` read this `shellcheckrc` file instead of running it with `--norc`. A relative path is resolved from the working directory, and the file must exist (exit status 3 otherwise). It overrides `shellcheck.rcfile` of the config file, which also has `args` and `enable`.

## EXIT STATUS

  * **0**:
    `jactionlint` ran and left no finding of level `error`. Findings of level `warn` and `info` do not count. The options that print something and exit (`--help`, `--version`, `--list-rules`, `--explain`, `--completion`, `--print-config-schema`, `--print-output-schema`) and a successful `--init-config`, `--migrate-config`, `--migrate-ignores` or `--baseline-write` exit with 0 too.

  * **1**:
    At least one finding of level `error` is left after the ignores, the baseline and `--min-severity`. With `--strict-exit` a finding of any level counts. `--diff` exits with 1 when its diff is not empty, and `--fix` when a finding that counts remains.

  * **2**:
    The command line is invalid: an unknown option, a missing or invalid value, an unknown rule ID, a regular expression of `--ignore` that does not compile, a combination of options that cannot work (such as `--online` with `--no-online`), or `--fix`, `--diff` or `--baseline-write` with `-`.

  * **3**:
    `jactionlint` failed: no repository was found, a file or a config file cannot be read or parsed, a baseline file, a `shellcheckrc` file or a command named by `--shellcheck`, `--pyflakes` or `--ruff` is missing, `--init-config` finds a config file already there, a fix was refused or did not converge, or `--online=strict` skipped a lookup (status 3 wins over 1).

The page https://jactionlint.jdx.dev/cli#exit-status lists every cause.

## ENVIRONMENT

  * `JACTIONLINT_ONLINE`:
    The default of `--online` and `--no-online`. `1`, `true` or `on` is `--online`. `cache`, `strict` and `cache,strict` are the modes of `--online=MODE`. `0`, `false`, `off` or `no` is `--no-online`. An empty value is not set, and any other value exits with status 2. The command line wins over the variable, and the variable wins over `online:` of the config file.

  * `JACTIONLINT_NO_HINTS`:
    The same as `--no-hints` when it is set to anything but an empty value, `0` or `false`.

  * `CI`, `GITHUB_ACTIONS`:
    Decide whether the hint line of a text run is printed when stderr is not a terminal. `CI` counts unless it is empty, `false` or `0`, and `GITHUB_ACTIONS` unless it is empty or `false`.

  * `NO_COLOR`:
    When it is set to a non-empty value, the output has no colors, also with `--color` and `--color=always`. `TERM=dumb` turns the colors off with `--color=auto` only.

  * `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `GH_TOKEN`:
    The token of the online checks, after `--online-token-env` and `--online-token-file`.

  * `GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN`, `GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN`:
    The token of the online checks on a GitHub Enterprise Server. They are read before `GITHUB_TOKEN` and `GH_TOKEN`.

  * `GITHUB_COM_TOKEN`:
    The token for `api.github.com` with `--online-github-com-fallback`.

  * `GITHUB_API_URL`, `GITHUB_SERVER_URL`, `GH_HOST`:
    The API host of the online checks when `--online-api-url` and the config file do not give one. `GITHUB_API_URL` is the API URL itself, and the other two name the host that it is derived from.

  * `GITHUB_GRAPHQL_URL`:
    The GraphQL endpoint that the rule `impostor-commit` uses to scan branches. It is ignored, with a notice, unless it is on the host of the REST API.

  * `GITHUB_REPOSITORY`:
    The repository (`owner/repo`) whose environments the online checks of `untrusted-checkout`, `untrusted-artifact` and `agentic-actions` look up. The default is the `origin` remote, when it is a github.com URL.

  * `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, `XDG_CACHE_HOME`:
    The base directories of the user-global config file and of the cache of the online checks. Only an absolute path counts, and the defaults are `~/.config` and `~/.cache`.

  * `SHELLCHECK_OPTS`:
    Read by `shellcheck` itself, also when `jactionlint` runs it with `--norc`.

  * `GIT_DIR`, `GIT_WORK_TREE`, `GIT_INDEX_FILE` and the other variables that redirect git:
    Removed from the environment of the `git` commands of `jactionlint`, so that a git hook cannot point them at another repository.

## FILES

  * `.github/workflows/*.yml`, `.github/workflows/*.yaml`:
    The workflow files of the repository.

  * `.github/jactionlint.yaml`, `.github/jactionlint.yml`:
    The config file of the repository. When neither exists, `jactionlint` reads `.github/actionlint.yaml` or `.github/actionlint.yml`, the names of actionlint. A file with such a name that sets no `profile` gets the `default` profile, which has more rules than actionlint, and `jactionlint` prints a note that says so.

  * `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/jactionlint/jactionlint.yaml`, `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/jactionlint/jactionlint.yml`:
    The user-global config file, used when the repository has none and `--config-file` is not given. `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/actionlint/actionlint.yaml` and `.yml` follow it. Without an absolute `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` the directory is `~/.config`. `jactionlint` uses the first file it finds and merges files only through `extends`. The page https://jactionlint.jdx.dev/config#configuration-file-location-and-priority has the details.

  * `.github/jactionlint-baseline.json`:
    The baseline that `--baseline-write` writes and `--baseline` reads.

  * `.github/workflows/actions.lock`:
    The Actions lock file, which `jactionlint` reads and compares with the workflows. It never writes it.

  * `.github/dependabot.yml`, `.github/dependabot.yaml`:
    The Dependabot configuration of the repository.

  * `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/jactionlint`:
    The disk cache of the online checks, at most 32 MiB (`~/.cache/jactionlint` without an absolute `XDG_CACHE_HOME`).

## EXAMPLES

Check the whole repository, only the given files, or a workflow that comes from stdin:

    jactionlint
    jactionlint .github/workflows/ci.yaml .github/workflows/release.yaml
    jactionlint - < workflow.yaml

Run only the checks of actionlint:

    jactionlint --profile correctness

Report only two rules, also when the profile does not enable them:

    jactionlint --select unpinned-uses,missing-timeout

Print what a rule checks, with its profile, level, options and fixes:

    jactionlint --explain unpinned-uses

Preview the safe fixes as a diff, then apply them:

    jactionlint --diff
    jactionlint --fix

Write SARIF for code scanning:

    jactionlint --format sarif > jactionlint.sarif

Record the current findings as the baseline, then fail only on new ones:

    jactionlint --baseline-write
    jactionlint --baseline

Run the online checks from the disk cache, without the network:

    jactionlint --online=cache

